// The 8-bit signedness face of decision 17's store rule: `i8 = <u8>` is refused
// even though both are 8 bits wide, and an explicit `as i8` is the escape.
//
// It reaches that refusal through the CARRIER clause, not the narrowing one.
// The original subject here was the range argument -- a u8 value can exceed
// i8's positive range (200 > 127) -- but a typed value keeps its own type
// across a store, so the carrier difference answers first and the range never
// gets asked. What survives of the narrowing obligation is pinned by
// narrowing_anonymous_expression_unproven, which needs a value with no carrier
// of its own to reach it. The wider same-width face is
// implicit_store_conversion_rejected (`u32` into `i32`).
use omega_language_std::console;
data Main { u: u8; s: i8; }
machine Main::main(main: &mut Main) {
    main.u = 200;
    main.s = main.u;
    let o: i32 = main.s as i32;
    Console::exit_process(o);
}
